              "body": "OUTPATIENT DERMATOLOGY\nDate of service: 03/12/2025\nReferred by: primary care\n\nReason for referral: patches on the trunk.\n\nHistory: 24-year-old woman with roughly three months of several irregular, faintly scaly spots on the trunk. They started out light brown and stood out more after a recent beach trip, when the skin around them tanned. Denies itch or discomfort.\n\nPast history: none. Medications: none. Allergies: no known drug allergies.\nFamily history: mother hypothyroidism; father hypertension. No melanoma or autoimmune skin disease in the family.\nSocial: graduate student, works as a lifeguard in summer. Never smoker; drinks rarely, 1-2 per month.\n\nExamination: Several salmon-pink and lighter-than-surrounding macules over the upper back and both shoulders, irregular in outline. Wood's lamp: yellowish fluorescence over the involved skin. Scrapings taken for KOH.\n\nKOH preparation: short, stubby hyphae with clusters of round spores.\n\nAssessment: Tinea versicolor (pityriasis versicolor).\n\nPlan:\n- Ketoconazole 2% shampoo: lather onto affected skin, leave 5 minutes, rinse. Daily for 2 weeks.\n- Discussed that pigment changes may persist for weeks to months after the yeast clears, and that recurrence in warm, humid weather is common.\n- Return as needed.\n\nThank you for the referral.\nDermatology"
            },
